WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:03.120
It’s hard to take a picture of a moving target

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:05.120
—just ask any parent.

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.140
Scientists have the same problem with plant roots

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:10.680
under the microscope.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:12.760
But now, one team has come up

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:14.180
with a unique technique

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.720
that lets microscopes automatically track

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:17.940
moving objects,

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:20.780
which they used to follow the growing roots

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.600
of these tiny plants.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:25.380
By using lasers and glowing proteins

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:28.260
to piece together images in three dimensions,

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:31.720
they even created 3D videos of how the cells

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:34.740
in root tips grow and split over the course of days.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:37.700
The team created a special lighting system

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:39.280
to keep the plants healthy,

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:41.080
and turned their microscope on its side

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.140
so the plants could grow upright.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.280
A rotating plate allowed them to study

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:47.220
how gravity changes root growth,

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:49.380
which could one day help scientists understand

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.020
the best ways to grow plants in space.

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:54.620
But the invention isn’t restricted to roots.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:57.760
The team also used this technique

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:59.340
with a different microscope

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:01.000
to watch how cells move around

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.000
in zebrafish embryos.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:05.480
And since they released their program for free,

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:07.500
other scientists can use it to capture

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:09.220
stunning new perspectives

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:11.480
of just about anything that moves.

